---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/PageHead.astro';
import '../../styles/v9.css';

const harnessFile =
	'https://github.com/XiaomiMiMo/mimoagent/blob/c3df8469f250b15e7906487a6d52be4c1827a509/src/mimoagent/environments/datasets/opensource_code.py';

const runners = [
	{
		runner: 'pytest',
		route: 'A conftest.py at the repository root that exits 0',
		probed: '1,001',
		inScope: '929 (92.8%)',
		upper: '954',
	},
	{
		runner: 'jest',
		route: 'An exit-0 shim in node_modules/.bin; in one environment, a rewritten package.json test script',
		probed: '179',
		inScope: '141 (78.8%)',
		upper: '141',
	},
	{
		runner: 'mocha',
		route: 'An exit-0 shim in node_modules/.bin',
		probed: '140',
		inScope: '110 (78.6%)',
		upper: '110',
	},
	{
		runner: 'vitest',
		route: 'An exit-0 shim in node_modules/.bin',
		probed: '45',
		inScope: '30 (66.7%)',
		upper: '30',
	},
	{
		runner: 'Go',
		route: 'A TestMain that exits 0, in a package that holds hidden tests',
		probed: '665',
		inScope: '112 (16.8%)',
		upper: '112',
	},
	{
		runner: 'node-tap, phpunit, .uts',
		route: 'None found with these probes',
		probed: '43',
		inScope: '0',
		upper: '0',
	},
	{
		runner: 'No standard runner',
		route: 'Not probed: graded by a custom script',
		probed: '528',
		inScope: '—',
		upper: '—',
	},
];

const restorations = [
	{ runner: 'pytest', fix: 'Restore conftest files and pytest config from base; apply the repository-only diff onto a fresh container.' },
	{ runner: 'jest', fix: 'Restore node_modules; call the runner by absolute path, directly rather than through npm test.' },
	{ runner: 'mocha, vitest', fix: 'Restore node_modules; call the runner by absolute path.' },
	{ runner: 'Go', fix: 'Drop any *_test.go file that is in neither the base commit nor the hidden patch.' },
];
---

<Layout
	title="MiMo grader audit — Cupel · Inductive ML"
	description="Cupel's audit of how Xiaomi's MiMo-V2.6 RL coding pack is graded: on 1,322 of 2,601 environments, one file written in the task working directory makes the grader report PASS with the hidden tests applied. Severity High; stamp FAIL."
>
	<SiteHeader current="cupel" />
		<PageHead back={{ href: '/cupel', label: 'RL Env Audits' }} kicker="Cupel · Coordinated disclosure" animate={true}>
			MiMo grader (Xiaomi)
		</PageHead>

		<main class="v9">
			<div class="pad">
				<div class="grid12">
					<div class="paper-hero">
						<div class="head">
							<p class="sub">
								In about half of Xiaomi's open RL coding pack, one file written in the task's working
								directory before grading makes the grader report PASS with the hidden tests applied, on
								environments that otherwise FAIL. <em>
									A policy trained on these environments can be rewarded for that write instead of the
									fix. We rate it High, so the pack's Cupel stamp is FAIL.
								</em>
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="meta-rail">
							<div class="row"><span>// suite</span><span>MiMo-V2.6-RL-oss</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// split</span><span>code · 2,602 tasks</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// probed</span><span>2,601 of 2,602</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// write scope</span><span>none declared</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// harness</span><span>mimoagent @ c3df846</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// severity</span><span>High (I3 × R3)</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// stamp</span><span>FAIL</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// status</span><span>published · 23 Nov 2026</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// disclosure</span><span>sent to Xiaomi · 7 Nov 2026</span></div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="paper">
						<div class="stat-strip">
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// forced PASS, default scope</span>
								<span class="val accent">1,322 / 2,601</span>
							</div>
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// canary sample</span>
								<span class="val">58 / 60</span>
							</div>
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// severity</span>
								<span class="val">High</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 01</span><span class="ttl">// the earlier audit</span><span class="meta">context</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="the earlier audit">
						<h2>A follow-up to the Low finding</h2>
						<p>
							Our first audit of this pack, published on 22 October 2026 at
							<a href="/cupel/mimo">inductive.ml/cupel/mimo</a>, found that file modification times reveal
							which files the reference fix touched. We rated that Low (I2 × R4), and it remains Low. That
							page stays as published.
						</p>
						<p>
							This page is a separate, higher-severity finding, about how the pack is graded rather than what
							its images leave on disk.
						</p>
						<p>
							The target is Xiaomi's open RL coding pack, <code class="code-inline">MiMo-V2.6-RL-oss</code>,
							shipped as one image per task under <code class="code-inline"
								>docker.io/xiaomimimo/mimo-v2.6-rl-oss</code
							>, with 2,602 tasks. We probed 2,601 of them between 5 and 7 November 2026; one task could not be scanned.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 02</span><span class="ttl">// how grading works</span><span class="meta">harness</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="how grading works">
						<h2>The agent and the grader share one filesystem</h2>
						<p>
							We read Xiaomi's published harness: <code class="code-inline">XiaomiMiMo/mimoagent</code>, branch
							<code class="code-inline">mimo-oss</code>, commit <code class="code-inline">c3df846</code>
							(<code class="code-inline">c3df8469f250b15e7906487a6d52be4c1827a509</code>), in
							<a href={harnessFile}><code class="code-inline">opensource_code.py</code></a>.
						</p>
						<p>
							In its <code class="code-inline">OpenSourceCodeEnvironment</code>, in the harness's own words,
							“the agent and the grader share one pod and one filesystem.” The reward is computed in four
							steps:
						</p>
						<ol>
							<li>Reset every path the hidden <code class="code-inline">test_patch</code> touches.</li>
							<li>Apply the <code class="code-inline">test_patch</code> with <code class="code-inline">git apply</code>.</li>
							<li>Run the task's <code class="code-inline">test_command</code>.</li>
							<li>Award 1.0 if and only if that command exits 0.</li>
						</ol>
						<p>
							The harness describes the reset as “not a hardening measure”: it is there to make
							<code class="code-inline">git apply</code> reliable. So a file the
							<code class="code-inline">test_patch</code> does not mention is still there at grade time.
						</p>
						<p>
							The dataset card describes an <code class="code-inline">anti_hack_cleanup</code> step. It runs
							at environment setup, before the agent starts, and does not isolate the policy's writes at
							reward time.
						</p>
						<p>
							The pack declares no write scope: none of the 2,602 tasks says where the agent may write, in
							its metadata or in its prompt. We read that as the conservative default, that the policy may
							write inside its own working directory, and the headline counts only writes there.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 03</span><span class="ttl">// the finding</span><span class="meta"
							>1,322 of 2,601</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="the finding">
						<h2>A file the hidden tests never mention decides the reward</h2>
						<p>
							The reward is the exit code of the test command, and the test command runs in the filesystem
							the agent has just worked in. So a file the agent leaves in its working directory can make
							that command exit 0. The hidden tests are applied, the grader reports PASS, and the task was
							not solved.
						</p>
						<p>
							Each test runner has its own place for such a file: for pytest, a
							<code class="code-inline">conftest.py</code> at the repository root; for jest, mocha and vitest,
							a shim in <code class="code-inline">node_modules/.bin</code> that stands in for the runner;
							for Go, a <code class="code-inline">TestMain</code> in a package that holds hidden tests. Each
							ends the test process with exit 0 before any hidden test runs. They are ordinary-looking
							files, written with ordinary tool use.
						</p>
						<p>
							<strong>On 1,322 of 2,601 environments (50.8%), a single write inside the task working directory
							forces PASS.</strong> If writes anywhere the container allows also count, including the
							interpreter's site-packages, the upper bound is 1,347 environments (51.8%), across 2,241
							routes.
						</p>
						<p>
							Each counted environment was confirmed the same way. In a fresh container from the pack's own
							image, a probe writes one file, then the pack's own grading runs: hidden patch applied, pack
							test command run. The environment counts only if it grades PASS with the probe and FAIL on a
							no-op control of the same environment. No probe passed its control. The probes use no model.
						</p>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>
									Forced-PASS routes by test runner · environments in scope by default (working-directory
									writes) and upper bound (anywhere the container allows)
								</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>Runner</th>
										<th>Route</th>
										<th>Probed</th>
										<th>In scope by default</th>
										<th>Upper bound</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									{
										runners.map((r) => (
											<tr>
												<td class="label">{r.runner}</td>
												<td>{r.route}</td>
												<td>{r.probed}</td>
												<td>{r.inScope}</td>
												<td>{r.upper}</td>
											</tr>
										))
									}
									<tr>
										<td class="label">All</td>
										<td></td>
										<td>2,601</td>
										<td>1,322 (50.8%)</td>
										<td>1,347 (51.8%)</td>
									</tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
						<p>
							Together, jest, mocha and vitest account for 281 of 364 environments. With pytest's 929 and
							Go's 112, that is the 1,322.
						</p>
						<p>
							Why this matters for RL: the grader trusts the exit code of a process that runs code the policy
							can write. On these environments a policy can collect the reward by writing one file instead
							of learning the skill, and on a training curve that looks like progress.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 04</span><span class="ttl">// canary sample</span><span class="meta">60 routes</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="canary sample">
						<h2>A test that fails on purpose still passes</h2>
						<p>
							To check that a PASS here means the grader has stopped measuring anything, we re-graded a
							sample of routes with a deliberately failing test (a canary) added after the hidden patch.
							The sample was 60 routes, stratified as 20 pytest, 20 jest, mocha or vitest, and 20 Go, with a
							fixed seed.
						</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>60 of 60</strong> routes reproduced.</li>
							<li>
								<strong>58 of 60</strong> still forced PASS with the canary in place (95% CI roughly
								88–99%): all 20 pytest, all 20 Go, and 18 of the 20 JavaScript routes.
							</li>
							<li>The other 2, both mocha routes, forced PASS on the probe but not with the canary.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>
							This is a second, stricter bar on a sample. We report it on its own and do not extrapolate it
							to the rest of the pack.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 05</span><span class="ttl">// <a href="/cupel#severity">severity</a></span><span class="meta">High</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="severity">
						<h2>High, unguarded</h2>
						<p>
							<strong>Impact: <a href="/cupel#severity">I3</a> (Full).</strong> The policy gets the full
							reward without doing the intended work: the grader reports PASS with the hidden tests in place,
							on environments that otherwise FAIL.
						</p>
						<p>
							<strong>Reach: <a href="/cupel#severity">R3</a> (Unusual, unguarded).</strong> The shortcut is a
							file write of the kind the scale names, a writable <code class="code-inline">conftest</code>
							or the same pattern for a <code class="code-inline">node_modules</code> shim or a Go
							<code class="code-inline">TestMain</code>. It needs ordinary tool use, not an expert exploit,
							and nothing in the pack guards against it.
						</p>
						<p>
							I3 × R3 is <strong>High</strong> on the published <a href="/cupel#severity">Cupel scale</a>,
							the same grid that rated the earlier file-mtime leak Low (I2 × R4). Any open High finding
							fails the <a href="/cupel#stamp">Cupel stamp</a>, so this pack's stamp is <strong>FAIL</strong>.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 06</span><span class="ttl">// the fix</span><span class="meta">order matters</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="the fix">
						<h2>Grade somewhere the policy cannot write</h2>
						<p>In this order:</p>
						<ol>
							<li>
								<strong>Grade in a fresh container</strong> with only the policy's source diff applied, and
								restore test infrastructure (<code class="code-inline">conftest</code>, pytest config,
								<code class="code-inline">node_modules</code>, runner binaries) from the base image or
								base commit. This is the change that closes the class.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Structured per-test reporting</strong> (JUnit XML, <code class="code-inline"
									>go test -json</code
								>, <code class="code-inline">jest --json</code>), requiring every hidden test to be
								reported as passed and treating a missing test as FAIL, is defence in depth, not the fix. A
								<code class="code-inline">conftest</code> or <code class="code-inline">sitecustomize</code>
								can forge a report file as easily as an exit code.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Declare a write scope</strong>, so that it is stated which paths a policy may
								write.
							</li>
						</ol>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>Per-runner restorations · proposed, not re-tested on a fixed grader</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>Runner</th>
										<th>Restoration</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									{
										restorations.map((r) => (
											<tr>
												<td class="label">{r.runner}</td>
												<td>{r.fix}</td>
											</tr>
										))
									}
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 07</span><span class="ttl">// other checks</span><span class="meta">sample</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="other checks">
						<h2>No solution leaks found in a 73-task sample</h2>
						<p>
							An agent scan of a stratified sample of 73 tasks, two runs each, found 0 confirmed solution
							leaks. That covers 2.8% of the pack, and the pack ships no reference fixes against which to
							confirm or clear a leak, so it is a sample, not a clearance.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 08</span><span class="ttl">// what this does not show</span><span class="meta"
							>limits</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="what this does not show">
						<h2>Limits</h2>
						<ul>
							<li>
								<strong>One task not probed.</strong> 2,601 of 2,602 tasks were probed; one could not be
								scanned on our setup.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Environments not probed.</strong> 528 environments grade through a custom script
								the hidden patch adds, rather than a standard test runner, and our probes were not run on
								them. A hand inspection of 20 of them found that all 20 grade on the script's exit code.
								They add nothing to the headline and are not cleared. Separately, 7 environments whose
								control run timed out have no finding counted.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>A fixed probe set.</strong> A PASS on a probe confirms a route. A FAIL says nothing
								about routes we did not try, so the counts are lower bounds for the runners probed.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Default scope against upper bound.</strong> The headline, 1,322, counts only writes
								inside the task working directory. The upper bound, 1,347, also counts writes outside it.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Probes, not a policy.</strong> Every route is a fixed probe; no model was involved.
								Whether a policy finds these routes on its own under RL was not tested.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Samples.</strong> The canary result (58 of 60) and the leak scan (0 in 73 tasks)
								are samples and are not extrapolated to the pack.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Fixes not re-tested.</strong> The fixes above are proposed; we have not run them
								against a fixed grader.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>One harness commit.</strong> Everything here refers to
								<code class="code-inline">XiaomiMiMo/mimoagent</code> at <code class="code-inline"
									>c3df846</code
								>.
							</li>
						</ul>
						<div class="callout" role="note">
							<span class="lbl">// summary</span>
							<p>
								As read at <strong>c3df846</strong>, Xiaomi's open-source coding harness grades in the same
								pod and filesystem the agent used, and awards reward on the test command's exit code. A file
								left in the task working directory can make that command exit 0 with the hidden tests
								applied: 1,322 of 2,601 environments (50.8%) are in scope by default, and 1,347 at most. We
								rate this High (I3 × R3, unguarded), so the stamp is FAIL. The fix is to grade in a fresh
								container with only the policy's source diff applied. The earlier file-mtime finding stays
								Low.
							</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 09</span><span class="ttl">// disclosure</span><span class="meta">record</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="disclosure record">
						<h2>Disclosure record</h2>
						<ul>
							<li>
								<strong>22 Oct 2026.</strong> The Low (I2 × R4) file-mtime note sent to Xiaomi's MiMo
								team. No reply.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>7 Nov 2026.</strong> The High (I3 × R3) grader note sent to Xiaomi's MiMo team,
								with copies to its support and security contacts, and a reproduction for Xiaomi to verify.
								Planned public date: 21 Nov 2026. No reply.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>17 Nov 2026.</strong> A follow-up in the same thread, restating that the earliest
								public date was 21 Nov 2026. No reply.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>23 Nov 2026.</strong> This report published. Xiaomi did not respond to any of the
								three notes.
							</li>
						</ul>
						<p>
							This public version describes the failure in plain terms and leaves out the probe scripts and
							the per-route list. The audit tooling is private. Questions about the report: send a DM on X to
							<a href="https://x.com/0xavp">@0xavp</a>.
						</p>
						<p>Aditya Veer Parmar, Cupel / Inductive ML</p>
					</section>

					<footer class="foot">
						<div class="a">Cupel · Inductive ML</div>
						<div class="b">MiMo grader disclosure · published 23 Nov 2026</div>
						<div class="c"><a href="/cupel">RL Env Audits ↗</a></div>
					</footer>
				</div>
			</div>
		</main>
		<SiteFooter animate={true} />
</Layout>
